      "marks": 4,
      "answer": "$C=(11,4)$ and $BC$ has gradient $0.5$ while $DC$ has gradient $-2$; hence $BC\\perp DC$, so $DC$ is tangent.",
      "official_mark_scheme_text": "$C=(11,4)$. B1\nGradient of $AB$, $BC$ or $AC$ is $0.5$. B1 — OE.\nGradient of $CD$ is $-2$. M1 — Calculation of gradient must be shown.\n$(0.5)(-2)=-1$, then states perpendicular, hence tangent. A1 — Clear reasoning needed.\nAlternative: $C=(11,4)$. B1; gradient of $AB$, $BC$ or $AC$ is $0.5$. B1; perpendicular equation $y-4=-2(x-11)$. M1 — Use $m_1m_2=-1$ and correct line method; may use $D(5,16)$. Check the other point lies on the line, or check gradient, then state tangent. A1.\nAlternative: $C=(11,4)$ or relevant radius gradient $0.5$. B1; perpendicular equation $y-4=-2(x-11)$. B1; substitute in circle to obtain $(x-11)^2=0$ or discriminant $0$. M1; state repeated root, hence tangent. A1.\nAlternative: $C=(11,4)$. B1; find $CD^2=180$ and $BD^2=225$. B1 — From coordinates without using $r$; check $(BD)^2-(CD)^2=r^2$. M1; Pythagoras gives perpendicular, hence tangent. A1.\nAlternative: $C=(11,4)$. B1; find correctly paired vectors $AC=(12,6)$ and $CD=(-6,12)$, or $BC=(6,3)$ and $CD=(-6,12)$. B1; apply scalar product. M1; scalar product $0$, hence perpendicular and tangent. A1.\nTotal: 4",
